I've got a few minutes after these very  stimulating and exciting and  inspirational presentations and  discussions to suggest that we think  about accessibility as we're thinking  about technology and teaching and  learning  when Peter Salovey became the president  of Yale he set forth a vision for the  university and he wants yell to be an  innovative Yale and I think we're seeing  evidence of that today two other goals  that he said are very dear and central  to the mission of the CTL to be the  research university most committed to  teaching and learning and to offer a  Yale education to an even more diverse  student body when he talks about this  mission this this second goal he talks  about an accessible Yale that opens its  doors based on individuals and  accomplishments potential and ability  what I'd like to ask is when we think  about this goal of diversity and our  commitment to diversity what is it that  we think about typically we tend to  think diversity means opening the doors  to students of racial ethnic maybe  socio-economic groups who have been  underrepresented at a place like Yale in  the past there are other groups that we  tend not to think about when we took  when we think about diversity another  question accessible when Peter salivate  uses this word accessible he's talking  about financial accessibility he wants  to make sure that anybody who is  qualified can access Yale regardless of  their financial means but what else  might we mean by accessible the  population that I'm suggesting that we  think about in terms of accessibility  and diversity are our students with  disabilities  so you might not realize this but  Americans  disabilities is one of the largest  minority populations in the u.s. there  are 57 million Americans with  disabilities which is roughly equal to  the number of Americans of Hispanic  Latino or Spanish origin descent  heritage and that makes up 19% of the  u.s. population in terms of the  population in among undergraduate  students 11% of undergraduate students  nationally as well as here at Yale  reported having at least one disability  but that number probably is undercounts  the total number of students who have a  disability here's an interesting  statistic of all the students who in  their K through 12 educational  environment received some type of  support from their schools only 1/3 of  those actually register when they go to  college with the office that is supposed  to support students with disabilities so  this suggests that of these 11% of  undergraduates who raise their hand and  self-identify as having a disability the  number is probably much larger in some  cases the student don't even realize  that they have a disability the largest  majority of these students have what we  might call invisible the disabilities so  when we tend to think about disability  we imagine a student who's blind and has  a guide dog or we imagine somebody in a  wheelchair but actually the most common  disabilities are largely invisible  interesting story I've had the  opportunity to speak with a number of  Yale faculty over the years about  students with disabilities and often  times I've heard them say you know I've  been teaching at Yale for 20 years I  don't think I've ever had a student in  my class with a disability and I told  them I'm sure that you have you just  weren't aware of it Yale students for  obvious reasons are  to raise their hands and self-identify  and ask for extra help of the students  who do report disabilities 36 percent of  them have learning disabilities  including dyslexia 26% have a DD ADHD  13% have mental illness or dealing with  certain psychological challenges and all  the other disabilities which are the  ones that we tend to think about the  most together make up 25% so this  graphic can can actually it actually  explains a bit why we're not thinking  about disabilities among our students  because we're not necessarily we can't  recognize the students who have  disabilities so technology it's been a  real boon for students with disabilities  it's it is allowing them more and more  to be independent and to rely less on  special accommodations that are provided  by an office devoted to that so some  examples for students with motor  disabilities and this can include  somebody who broke her arm as well as  somebody who uses wheelchair somebody  with with a spinal cord injury who might  not be able to use their hands there are  special keyboards that make it easier  for them to keep up with writing  assignments they're also new and  increasingly good speech-to-text  technology so rather than the student  typing a paper there was actually a law  student here who wrote and submitted his  entire thesis using this technology  where he trained a computer program to  understand his voice so that he could  dictate and correct not using his hands  at all it's pretty amazing  for students that have auditory  disabilities captioning and transcripts  are just critical as more and more  faculty are using media in their  teaching and learning we also have the  service for for students who have  hearing difficulties there can be live  captioning going on during a lecture  we have a service that's available you  might not be aware of it if you're  teaching in the classroom and you see a  student with a laptop open what what we  can do is there will be somebody it's  basically like a court reporter who is  is doing a live transcription of your  lecture and that's being sent through  Wireless to the students laptop so the  student can watch and read and follow  your lecture even if they're not sitting  close enough to lip-read or they they  can't hear for for another reason one  last example when I talked about speech  to text there's also text-to-speech  technology so a student who can't read  by looking at the text increasingly use  technology that can look at the text for  the student and actually translate it  into into sound and one thing I'll point  out here that that it is actually very  illuminating this technology was first  developed for students with visual  disabilities somebody who needs who  can't see the text but can listen to it  what's really interesting is that the  technologies technology that was  developed for one population actually is  very useful for another population and  and this is students with dyslexia who  learn much better if they can hear  something rather than only read it or  can hear it at the same time that  they're eating it so this is an example  of the idea of universal design the idea  is that if you can if we can find a way  to create an access for one population  that that access point might actually be  beneficial to populations you didn't  even anticipate this is the classic  example if I asked for a show of hands  of how many of you have ever pushed a  baby stroller down the sidewalk or  rolled your luggage cart down the  sidewalk or taking a bicycle on the  sidewalk how many of you have ax  looked for the ramp so that it made your  life much easier I see lots of hands of  course these ramps were not designed to  make it easier for you to roll your  luggage maybe at the airport that's the  case but typically these ramps were  designed to help people who use  wheelchairs but this technology this  design that that was meant to meet the  needs of one particular population  actually makes life a lot easier for  everybody so this is the goal when we  think about how can we make our courses  most accessible can we find the  educational equivalents of these curb  cuts so that we are thinking proactively  about the needs of a particular  population in our course but we're  actually making our course more  accessible to everybody  so one of the challenges were technology  even though it can offer a lot of  support it can also introduce obstacles  and this is why whenever we choose a  technology and whenever our students  become very dependent on a technology we  need to be very thoughtful and  deliberate in the technologies that that  we use we can unwittingly  by selecting a certain technology make  our make a learning experience  inaccessible to some of our students a  good example we have the teal classroom  up on hill house and it's designed to  promote active learning  we had an instructor a couple of years  ago who was following all the best  practices for how to lead an active  classroom and discussion based in small  groups what she didn't anticipate was  that a deaf student enrolled in the  course and this duct student was very  very good at lip-reading  but in a discussion based classroom when  everybody is talking at the same time  and when the professor is walking around  the room and talking the student had no  idea who to look at and she could not  keep up so she actually went to the  professor and said I really want to  take this class but the way that you've  designed in this active learning is  making it inaccessible to me the  professor was horrified she was trying  to do all the right things and she had  never really thought oh what if I have a  deaf student in my class so the  challenge is trying to think proactively  to imagine the diversity of students who  might enroll in your class and rather  than be in the position that this  professor was in was after doing months  of planning and rolling it out all of a  sudden having to rethink everything on  the spot quickly can we be more  proactive and more deliberate and  thoughtful about the way we design our  classes so that we're not discriminating  against certain students and we are  providing as many as many curb cuts as  possible so it's just a question if  anybody most people here are using the  learning management system any any  thoughts on what might be the most  common accessibility issue we have in  canvas  any yeah I guess is one thing I'll  mention so we just we just completed  this transition to canvas it was painful  it took a lot of effort from everybody  but one of the main reasons we did it  was because our old system classes b2  was very very bad at being accessible to  people who use a screen reader canvas is  excellent to that okay I'll give you the  answer inaccessible PDF files so faculty  are increasingly sharing readings with  their students by scanning a textbook or  an article and uploading a PDF file into  canvas problem is if you don't create  the PDF in the right way a student who's  dyslexic and using a screen reader or a  student who has visual impairment will  not be able to access that so there's a  lot that we debt that we're still  learning there's a lot that we don't  know that we're doing that is making it  more difficult for students so I'm very  happy that this month in fact on  Wednesday we are  getting off a new series through the CTL  where we're going to be thinking about  issues issues of accessibility in course  design and in the use of technology and  there is a handout on the table read out  here that highlights a whole series of  events going on throughout the month of  October October is National Disability  Awareness Month seven of those are  actually offered by the CTL and they're  highlighted here so I invite you to to  come to any that you can we hope to  continue the series beyond October and  we look forward to working with you  thank you  [Laughter]  [Music]
 

